{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"head -28 ../agent-source/go.mod; nl -ba ../agent-source/storage.go | sed -n '95,130p;205,240p'; nl -ba ../agent-source/status.go | sed -n '25,65p'; nl -ba ../agent-source/README.md | sed -n '112,126p'; cat /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/resources/skills/evidence-review/references/experiments.md | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"module go.etcd.io/raft/v3\n\ngo 1.26\n\ntoolchain go1.26.7\n\nrequire (\n\tgithub.com/cockroachdb/datadriven v1.0.2\n\tgithub.com/gogo/protobuf v1.3.2\n\tgithub.com/golang/protobuf v1.5.4\n\tgithub.com/stretchr/testify v1.10.0\n)\n\nrequire (\n\tgithub.com/davecgh/go-spew v1.1.1 // indirect\n\tgithub.com/google/go-cmp v0.5.8 // indirect\n\tgithub.com/pmezard/go-difflib v1.0.0 // indirect\n\tgoogle.golang.org/protobuf v1.33.0 // indirect\n\tgopkg.in/yaml.v3 v3.0.1 // indirect\n)\n    95\t\n    96\t// MemoryStorage implements the Storage interface backed by an\n    97\t// in-memory array.\n    98\ttype MemoryStorage struct {\n    99\t\t// Protects access to all fields. Most methods of MemoryStorage are\n   100\t\t// run on the raft goroutine, but Append() is run on an application\n   101\t\t// goroutine.\n   102\t\tsync.Mutex\n   103\t\n   104\t\thardState pb.HardState\n   105\t\tsnapshot  pb.Snapshot\n   106\t\t// ents[i] has raft log position i+snapshot.Metadata.Index\n   107\t\tents []pb.Entry\n   108\t\n   109\t\tcallStats inMemStorageCallStats\n   110\t}\n   111\t\n   112\t// NewMemoryStorage creates an empty MemoryStorage.\n   113\tfunc NewMemoryStorage() *MemoryStorage {\n   114\t\treturn &MemoryStorage{\n   115\t\t\t// When starting from scratch populate the list with a dummy entry at term zero.\n   116\t\t\tents: make([]pb.Entry, 1),\n   117\t\t}\n   118\t}\n   119\t\n   120\t// InitialState implements the Storage interface.\n   121\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) InitialState() (pb.HardState, pb.ConfState, error) {\n   122\t\tms.callStats.initialState++\n   123\t\treturn ms.hardState, ms.snapshot.Metadata.ConfState, nil\n   124\t}\n   125\t\n   126\t// SetHardState saves the current HardState.\n   127\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) SetHardState(st pb.HardState) error {\n   128\t\tms.Lock()\n   129\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   130\t\tms.hardState = st\n   205\t// ApplySnapshot overwrites the contents of this Storage object with\n   206\t// those of the given snapshot.\n   207\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) ApplySnapshot(snap pb.Snapshot) error {\n   208\t\tms.Lock()\n   209\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   210\t\n   211\t\t//handle check for old snapshot being applied\n   212\t\tmsIndex := ms.snapshot.Metadata.Index\n   213\t\tsnapIndex := snap.Metadata.Index\n   214\t\tif msIndex >= snapIndex {\n   215\t\t\treturn ErrSnapOutOfDate\n   216\t\t}\n   217\t\n   218\t\tms.snapshot = snap\n   219\t\tms.ents = []pb.Entry{{Term: snap.Metadata.Term, Index: snap.Metadata.Index}}\n   220\t\treturn nil\n   221\t}\n   222\t\n   223\t// CreateSnapshot makes a snapshot which can be retrieved with Snapshot() and\n   224\t// can be used to reconstruct the state at that point.\n   225\t// If any configuration changes have been made since the last compaction,\n   226\t// the result of the last ApplyConfChange must be passed in.\n   227\tfunc (ms *MemoryStorage) CreateSnapshot(i uint64, cs *pb.ConfState, data []byte) (pb.Snapshot, error) {\n   228\t\tms.Lock()\n   229\t\tdefer ms.Unlock()\n   230\t\tif i <= ms.snapshot.Metadata.Index {\n   231\t\t\treturn pb.Snapshot{}, ErrSnapOutOfDate\n   232\t\t}\n   233\t\n   234\t\toffset := ms.ents[0].Index\n   235\t\tif i > ms.lastIndex() {\n   236\t\t\tgetLogger().Panicf(\"snapshot %d is out of bound lastindex(%d)\", i, ms.lastIndex())\n   237\t\t}\n   238\t\n   239\t\tms.snapshot.Metadata.Index = i\n   240\t\tms.snapshot.Metadata.Term = ms.ents[i-offset].Term\n    25\t// The Progress is only populated on the leader.\n    26\ttype Status struct {\n    27\t\tBasicStatus\n    28\t\tConfig   tracker.Config\n    29\t\tProgress map[uint64]tracker.Progress\n    30\t}\n    31\t\n    32\t// BasicStatus contains basic information about the Raft peer. It does not allocate.\n    33\ttype BasicStatus struct {\n    34\t\tID uint64\n    35\t\n    36\t\tpb.HardState\n    37\t\tSoftState\n    38\t\n    39\t\tApplied uint64\n    40\t\n    41\t\tLeadTransferee uint64\n    42\t}\n    43\t\n    44\tfunc getProgressCopy(r *raft) map[uint64]tracker.Progress {\n    45\t\tm := make(map[uint64]tracker.Progress)\n    46\t\tr.trk.Visit(func(id uint64, pr *tracker.Progress) {\n    47\t\t\tp := *pr\n    48\t\t\tp.Inflights = pr.Inflights.Clone()\n    49\t\t\tpr = nil\n    50\t\n    51\t\t\tm[id] = p\n    52\t\t})\n    53\t\treturn m\n    54\t}\n    55\t\n    56\tfunc getBasicStatus(r *raft) BasicStatus {\n    57\t\ts := BasicStatus{\n    58\t\t\tID:             r.id,\n    59\t\t\tLeadTransferee: r.leadTransferee,\n    60\t\t}\n    61\t\ts.HardState = r.hardState()\n    62\t\ts.SoftState = r.softState()\n    63\t\ts.Applied = r.raftLog.applied\n    64\t\treturn s\n    65\t}\n   112\tAfter creating a Node, the user has a few responsibilities:\n   113\t\n   114\tFirst, read from the Node.Ready() channel and process the updates it contains. These steps may be performed in parallel, except as noted in step 2.\n   115\t\n   116\t1. Write Entries, HardState and Snapshot to persistent storage in order, i.e. Entries first, then HardState and Snapshot if they are not empty. If persistent storage supports atomic writes then all of them can be written together. Note that when writing an Entry with Index i, any previously-persisted entries with Index >= i must be discarded.\n   117\t\n   118\t2. Send all Messages to the nodes named in the To field. It is important that no messages be sent until the latest HardState has been persisted to disk, and all Entries written by any previous Ready batch (Messages may be sent while entries from the same batch are being persisted). To reduce the I/O latency, an optimization can be applied to make leader write to disk in parallel with its followers (as explained at section 10.2.1 in Raft thesis). If any Message has type MsgSnap, call Node.ReportSnapshot() after it has been sent (these messages may be large). Note: Marshalling messages is not thread-safe; it is important to make sure that no new entries are persisted while marshalling. The easiest way to achieve this is to serialise the messages directly inside the main raft loop.\n   119\t\n   120\t3. Apply Snapshot (if any) and CommittedEntries to the state machine. If any committed Entry has Type EntryConfChange, call Node.ApplyConfChange() to apply it to the node. The configuration change may be cancelled at this point by setting the NodeID field to zero before calling ApplyConfChange (but ApplyConfChange must be called one way or the other, and the decision to cancel must be based solely on the state machine and not external information such as the observed health of the node).\n   121\t\n   122\t4. Call Node.Advance() to signal readiness for the next batch of updates. This may be done at any time after step 1, although all updates must be processed in the order they were returned by Ready.\n   123\t\n   124\tSecond, all persisted log entries must be made available via an implementation of the Storage interface. The provided MemoryStorage type can be used for this (if repopulating its state upon a restart), or a custom disk-backed implementation can be supplied.\n   125\t\n   126\tThird, after receiving a message from another node, pass it to Node.Step:\nEXECUTION Observe actual implementation effects under explicit inputs, caller policy and authorized isolation. Contract applicability may remain unresolved during exploration; execution permission may not. A changed value or tests_passed is not a violation oracle. Exploration remains construction knowledge: a later requirement needs a separately fixed check, fresh execution and correspondence review, not retroactive relabelling. Missing capability, compilation failure, timeout, panic and a completed property comparison remain distinct. Retain failures; never precompute expected observations or alter protocol behavior to create a finding.\n\nCOMPLETION Separate the proposition's observation endpoint, necessary caller completion, and resource cleanup. A normal nonzero test exit may leave a completed violating witness intact only through checker_correspondence.execution_attribution binding the exact target CheckRun, checker and valid event indices, retained failure stream lines and fixed harness lines. Use source_ids and rationale to justify that all duties needed by that witness completed before an independent failure, with no corruption of history, identity or values. Machine validation checks these references and eligibility, not causal independence; stdout/stderr line numbers are not a common clock. Ordinary no_issue_found never authorizes nonzero exits. Preserve raw failure, other scenario debts and later challenges; no holds-only, timeout, cancellation, signal, build failure, missing premise, ambiguous or contradictory observation can use this path. Do not remove required join/close, ignore exceptions or kill workers after printing to manufacture PASS; unsupported short wall-clock limits are not extra properties. Changed harnesses, premises, predicates or scope still need the existing new-artifact and actual-execution path. Initialization, driver compliance and observation validity belong in prerequisites, controls or diagnostics, not duplicate properties. Check a wait's return or record actual attainment when it establishes a necessary premise; when waiting is the investigated behavior, requiring success would discard the failure history. Sleep establishes elapsed time, not synchronization, completion or a coherent snapshot; a printed readiness flag is not a prerequisite witness. A diagnostic control need not satisfy the principal scenario's premises or acquire a separate obligation: select formal applicability from independently declared input/policy fields before execution, retaining control output. Distinct formal scenarios need their actual admission and premises; incompatible histories may need separate checks. Never select scenarios by observed results or missing premises after execution. Role readiness does not imply that earlier work drained. Across preparation, policy changes and delivery, preserve caller/transport duties; prefer one policy-selected delivery path and record the identity, provenance and order needed for attribution. Distinguish receipt, per-message-type queueing and dispatch; preserve the replaced layer's ordering without assuming global FIFO or arbitrary reordering. Legal in-flight work need not be emptied. End controlled work independently of the forbidden behavior. Observe admission, completion and final state for the same operation before treating absence as bounded holds. Missing completion, identity, result events and tool timeout remain unknown. A finite window records non-completion, not a deadline violation or infinite stalling. A repeatable suffix must account for behavior-determining state, schedule, queues, timers and callbacks; equal business fields do not close execution. A rescue stimulus may itself be required caller work.\n\nCORRELATION Monitor.admission_alias selects the declared prerequisite admitting operations whose completion is owed, not the first array entry. Harness.prerequisites contains prerequisites only; monitors select independent results. During construction, compare ObservableProperty.identity_fields with the actual prerequisite and result events together. Shared setup may support multiple operations through explicit plan relationships; neither duplicate identical events mechanically nor rely on controller-supplied identity. Do not repeat results as prerequisites or each identity equality as a Comparison. References declare predecessor relationships; unrelated prerequisites have no implicit order. Assertions may use any declared alias actually preceding the result. Missing identity/reference fields or nonunique associations remain unknown. Independent applicability precedes unrelated history requirements; alias-dependent applicability follows correlation. Match operations separately. A complete violating witness survives missing results in demonstrably independent operations with partial coverage; same-witness ambiguity or potentially global parse loss blocks attribution. Process success, observed differences and complete correlation are separate facts. Associate input and output by independent identity, not compared values or a context expected to change. Unsupported temporal expressions remain an explicit limitation.\n\nMEANING Distinguish the written obligation, the proposition encoded by applicability/trigger/antecedent/assertion, and what this execution actually compared. Type, count or numeric-position equality may be necessary without establishing content or identity equality. Record comparisons and scope limits separately. Identify live object, copy, cache or caller-maintained observations, their writers and the synchronization or ownership boundary supporting the read; multiple fields must represent the state the proposition needs. Include ownership of injection flags, counters and sampling itself. An export discrepancy need not mean active protocol state changed. Match observation to the claim: stopping may change the behavior; neither shutdown sampling, locks, public APIs, dual reads nor race detection are universally required. Challenge specific unreliable reads, not all concurrent checks. Keep unresolved contracts, counterevidence, input legality and observation differences explicit; CheckRun owns execution status. An implementation/requirement discrepancy is the subject, not a conflict between requirement sources. Real contradictions remain conflicts.\n\nCONFIRMATION Reconstruct decisive relationships from the fixed test, actual constructors/producers and raw events; the plan's legality account is a claim to verify. Check prepared state, substituted calls and identity/context/generation across stages; constructible inputs or isolated fragments do not establish one legal history. Preserve relevant queues, callbacks, lock ownership and caller/transport duties. For waiting, missing replies or retained resources, trace waiter, result/cancel/close producer, normal shutdown entry and the observation's shutdown phase. Verify which owner/caller actually performs cleanup: no local timeout does not prove permanent waiting, and an available Close method does not prove it ran. A direct post-stop handler call differs from production dispatch still reaching it. Sourced local interface or exit duties need no whole-deployment proof. Review oracle, parsing, reached premises and completion together. Use rationale, source_ids, counterevidence, challenged_components and exact ReviewIssues for discrepancies; choose driver repair, encoding or F2/F3 by what changes, never weaken the written responsibility to rescue confirmation. Incompatible inputs are attribution gaps. Local functions, injectable storage, bounded initialization and substitute schedules remain valid when the sourced proposition permits them. Preserve measurements while correspondence is pending; complete local counterexamples survive independent wider-history or endpoint limits. New source counterevidence may challenge prior confirmation; an unobserved wider endpoint remains a limit.\nNECESSITY For S implies E, compare S when independently established E is false, or use equivalent supported implication encoding; never strengthen to S equals E or filter the result through applicability. In a complete implication comparison, observed S=false retains holds but supports only that S did not occur here, not E or universal consistency; S=true actually tests E. Missing results, identity or prerequisites are not observed falsity and cannot supply holds. Do not require every conditional branch to execute before confirming a complete local counterexample, or infer eventual success from a permitted pending control.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"The captured docs do not clearly require ApplyConfChange before an early Advance, but RawNode.Advance’s stricter wording remains unresolved. I’ll submit a conditional exploration using a removal entry generated by a real election and replication prefix, then replay it through public Node calls. This can test dispatch and campaign admission; it cannot settle the contract or establish a defect.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > explore_advance_test.go <<'GO'\npackage raft\n\nimport (\n \\\"context\\\"\n \\\"encoding/json\\\"\n \\\"fmt\\\"\n \\\"testing\\\"\n \\\"time\\\"\n\n pb \\\"go.etcd.io/raft/v3/raftpb\\\"\n)\n\nfunc assuranceEmit(v map[string]interface{}) {\n b, err := json.Marshal(v); if err \"'!= nil { panic(err) }; fmt.Println(\"CA_EVENT \" + string(b))\n}\nfunc assuranceConfig(id uint64, s *MemoryStorage) *Config {\n return &Config{ID:id, ElectionTick:10, HeartbeatTick:1, Storage:s, Applied:1, MaxSizePerMsg:4096, MaxInflightMsgs:16}\n}\nfunc assurancePersist(t *testing.T, s *MemoryStorage, rd Ready) {\n t.Helper()\n if !IsEmptySnap(rd.Snapshot) { if err:=s.ApplySnapshot(rd.Snapshot); err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)} }\n if len(rd.Entries)>0 {if err:=s.Append(rd.Entries);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}}\n if !IsEmptyHardState(rd.HardState) {if err:=s.SetHardState(rd.HardState);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}}\n}\nfunc assuranceApplyRaw(t *testing.T, r *RawNode, entries []pb.Entry) {\n t.Helper()\n for _,e:=range entries {\n  switch e.Type {\n  case pb.EntryConfChange:\n   var cc pb.ConfChange; if err:=cc.Unmarshal(e.Data);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};r.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n  case pb.EntryConfChangeV2:\n   var cc pb.ConfChangeV2; if err:=cc.Unmarshal(e.Data);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};r.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n  }\n }\n}\n// Generate the removal with actual participants and persist-before-send Ready handling.\n// The initial snapshot is the explicitly selected initial cluster, not an invented election.\nfunc assuranceRemovalPrefix(t *testing.T) (*MemoryStorage,uint64) {\n rs:=map[uint64]*RawNode{};ss:=map[uint64]*MemoryStorage{}\n for id:=uint64(1);id<=3;id++ {\n  s:=NewMemoryStorage();if err:=s.ApplySnapshot(pb.Snapshot{Metadata:pb.SnapshotMetadata{Index:1,Term:1,ConfState:pb.ConfState{Voters:[]uint64{1,2,3}}}});err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n  if err:=s.SetHardState(pb.HardState{Term:1,Commit:1});err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n  r,err:=NewRawNode(assuranceConfig(id,s));if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};rs[id]=r;ss[id]=s\n }\n pump:=func(){\n  for round:=0;round<100;round++ {\n   var queue []pb.Message;work:=false\n   for id:=uint64(1);id<=3;id++ {\n    r:=rs[id];if !r.HasReady(){continue};work=true;rd:=r.Ready();assurancePersist(t,ss[id],rd)\n    queue=append(queue,rd.Messages...)\n    assuranceApplyRaw(t,r,rd.CommittedEntries);r.Advance(rd)\n   }\n   for _,msg:=range queue {if err:=rs[msg.To].Step(msg);err!=nil && err!=ErrStepPeerNotFound {t.Fatal(err)}}\n   if !work && len(queue)==0{return}\n  }\n  t.Fatal(\"prefix failed to drain within diagnostic round bound\")\n }\n if err:=rs[2].Campaign();err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};pump()\n if rs[2].BasicStatus().RaftState!=StateLeader {t.Fatal(\"prefix did not elect node 2\")}\n if err:=rs[2].ProposeConfChange(pb.ConfChange{Type:pb.ConfChangeRemoveNode,NodeID:1});err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};pump()\n hs,_,err:=ss[1].InitialState();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n first,err:=ss[1].FirstIndex();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};last,err:=ss[1].LastIndex();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n ents,err:=ss[1].Entries(first,last+1,''^uint64(0));if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n var removal uint64\n for _,e:=range ents {if e.Type==pb.EntryConfChange {var cc pb.ConfChange;if err:=cc.Unmarshal(e.Data);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};if cc.Type==pb.ConfChangeRemoveNode && cc.NodeID==1 {removal=e.Index}}}\n if removal==0 || hs.Commit<removal {t.Fatal(\"node 1 did not durably learn committed removal\")}\n assuranceEmit(map[string]interface{}{\"event\":\"prefix\",\"node\":1,\"leader\":2,\"term\":hs.Term,\"commit\":hs.Commit,\"removal_index\":removal})\n return ss[1],removal\n}\nfunc assuranceClone(t *testing.T,s *MemoryStorage)*MemoryStorage {\n snap,err:=s.Snapshot();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};hs,_,err:=s.InitialState();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n first,err:=s.FirstIndex();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};last,err:=s.LastIndex();if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n ents,err:=s.Entries(first,last+1,''^uint64(0));if err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n out:=NewMemoryStorage();if err:=out.ApplySnapshot(snap);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};if err:=out.Append(ents);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};if err:=out.SetHardState(hs);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};return out\n}\nfunc TestAssuranceAdvanceBeforeConfigurationExploration(t *testing.T) {\n prefix,removal:=assuranceRemovalPrefix(t)\n for _,early:=range []bool{false,true} {\n  t.Run(fmt.Sprintf(\"early_%v\",early),func(t *testing.T){\n   // Restore application state from the initial snapshot, replaying persisted log entries.\n   s:=assuranceClone(t,prefix);n:=RestartNode(assuranceConfig(1,s));defer n.Stop()\n   ctx,cancel:=context.WithTimeout(context.Background(),5*time.Second);defer cancel()\n   var rd Ready\n   select {case rd=<-n.Ready():case <-ctx.Done():t.Fatal(\"replay Ready unavailable\")}\n   assurancePersist(t,s,rd)\n   var cc pb.ConfChange;found:=false\n   for _,e:=range rd.CommittedEntries {if e.Index==removal {if err:=cc.Unmarshal(e.Data);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)};found=true}}\n   if !found || cc.NodeID!=1 || cc.Type!=pb.ConfChangeRemoveNode {t.Fatal(\"replay did not contain expected removal\")}\n   before:=n.Status()\n   // Control campaigns while the unadvanced Ready remains pending. The conditional\n   // schedule acknowledges that same Ready before installing its configuration.\n   if early {n.Advance()}\n   if err:=n.Campaign(ctx);err!=nil {t.Fatal(err)}\n   after:=n.Status() // actor-owned coherent copy, after campaign dispatch\n   _,eligible:=after.Config.Voters[0][1]\n   assuranceEmit(map[string]interface{}{\"event\":\"campaign_observation\",\"early_advance\":early,\"node\":1,\"removal_index\":removal,\"before_term\":before.Term,\"after_term\":after.Term,\"before_applied\":before.Applied,\"after_applied\":after.Applied,\"commit\":after.Commit,\"state\":after.RaftState.String(),\"self_in_config\":eligible})\n   // Complete the delayed work before shutdown; no result requires the delay to persist.\n   conf:=n.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n   if !early {n.Advance()}\n   // Persist any resulting term/vote update and drop network messages under the\n   // explicitly isolated suffix. No response is fabricated or fed back.\n   final:=n.Status()\n   if early && final.Term!=before.Term {\n    select {case extra:=<-n.Ready():assurancePersist(t,s,extra);if len(extra.CommittedEntries)>0 {t.Fatal(\"unexpected new application work\")};n.Advance();case <-ctx.Done():t.Fatal(\"missing campaign Ready\")}\n   }\n   final=n.Status()\n   assuranceEmit(map[string]interface{}{\"event\":\"completion\",\"early_advance\":early,\"node\":1,\"removal_index\":removal,\"applied\":final.Applied,\"voters\":conf.Voters,\"state\":final.RaftState.String()})\n  })\n }\n}\nGO\n/usr/bin/python3 - <<'\"'PY'\nimport json\ns={'action':'explore','question':'Under the conditional reading that Node.Advance may precede ApplyConfChange during ongoing application, does a real persisted removal replay permit public Node.Campaign to enter candidacy before the removal is installed, compared with an unadvanced Ready?','rationale':'Resolve actual production dispatch and replay reachability using a generated election/replication prefix. This is construction knowledge only: early-Advance legality remains disputed by stricter RawNode wording. All persistence precedes outgoing prefix messages, all prefix configurations are applied, replay restores the old application snapshot, and delayed configuration work finishes before Stop. The isolated suffix may drop network traffic; no election victory or safety violation is claimed.','harness_path':'explore_advance_test.go','sources':[{'id':'S-restart-contract','file':'node.go','start_line':282,'end_line':293,'kind':'interface_statement'},{'id':'S-public-campaign','file':'node.go','start_line':472,'end_line':484,'kind':'code_observation'},{'id':'S-status-observation','file':'status.go','start_line':25,'end_line':64,'kind':'code_observation'},{'id':'S-memory-recovery','file':'storage.go','start_line':205,'end_line':226,'kind':'interface_statement'}],'feedback':{'ref_ids':['b53d1b5d808543a78a44be6432d23027','S-node-advance-contract','S-raw-advance','S-hup','S-restart-contract'],'answered':'Captured caller documentation permits Advance during application, and RestartNode explicitly supports replay from the last applied snapshot/index. No inspected statement explicitly orders ApplyConfChange before early Advance; RawNode.Advance remains contrary stricter wording. The exploration fixes a conditional schedule rather than resolving that normative ambiguity.','remaining':['Whether general early-Advance permission encompasses configuration installation.','Actual campaign admission and completion observations from the submitted public-Node replay.'],'rationale':'A generated prefix and public dispatch distinguish real reachability from a direct private-state mutation without claiming legality from tool success.'}}\njson.dump(s,open('submission-explore-advance.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_08-49-50-etcd_raft-real-run --submission submission-explore-advance.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
